# Every operator PLRM 8.2 defines, with the errors the specification gives it.
#
# Derived from the specification, not from this interpreter: the point of
# the file is to be the one side of the comparison that xpost cannot move.
# COMPLIANCE says what this interpreter does about each; a guard holds that
# document to this list, so an operator the specification defines and the
# document never mentions is a failure rather than a silence.
#
# That silence is what this exists for. COMPLIANCE named 318 operators and
# the specification defines rather more, and the difference was not visible
# from inside the document: nothing there could say what was missing from
# it. Every one of the absent ones had in fact been swept and tested; none
# had been written up.
#
# Format: one operator per line, as
#     <category> <TAB> <name> <TAB> <errors, comma-separated, or ->
#
# The categories are the specification's own (PLRM 8.1), which is how the
# operators were swept. They are not language levels; COMPLIANCE groups by
# level, and the two groupings do not nest. An entry with no errors of its
# own carries -.
#
# A line beginning `absent' names an operator this interpreter
# deliberately does not make reachable, and why. The guard requires the
# set it cannot reach to be exactly this set: an operator that goes
# missing fails, and one that arrives and is still listed here fails too.
